<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=alternating-current">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alternating current</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as AC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alternating current is an electric current that periodically reverses direction, the form in which electric power is delivered to homes and businesses. The French instrument maker Hippolyte Pixii built the first alternator to produce it in 1832, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a>'s principles. In 1885 Károly Zipernowsky, Ottó Bláthy and Miksa Déri of the Ganz Works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> patented closed-core transformers, and their system of 75 transformers supplied 1,067 lamps at the Hungarian National Exhibition that May. Westinghouse then built AC systems across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the first three-phase system was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1891.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1832</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Hippolyte Pixii</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Pixii's alternator came first, but alternating current became practical for power only with the transformers of the 1880s; from late 1887 Thomas Edison campaigned against it as too dangerous in the war of the currents.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">Electric generator<span>1831</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electromagnetic-induction">Electromagnetic induction<span>1831</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=alternating-current">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
